Common mistakes include treating estate management as only sales and agency. It is also valuation, law, planning, finance, management and public policy.

Other errors are:
using a rule from one country as if it were universal;
quoting a code, tax rate or fee without checking the current official source;
confusing price, rent, value and yield;
forgetting that a valuation is an opinion with a stated basis, date and assumptions;
ignoring lease terms, planning conditions or maintenance costs;
copying a spreadsheet result without checking the inputs.

In exams and reports, state your assumptions and show your working. A clear method earns marks even when the final figure is imperfect.
